{
	title: 'Commit cards: a commit a Bash call makes is a card beside its result',
	spec: 'When a Bash call (foreground or background) creates commits (detection: task hy, before the result is written), the history record that carries its result also carries one commit block per commit, after the tool_result: hash, short hash, subject, amend flag, repository directory, files with lines added and removed (one git show --numstat per commit, only when commits exist) and lines. Its id is the record number with a sub-index (e.g. #s357.1, task 9p letter s); no other record carries it, so replay, older pages and links show it as recorded. The transcript draws it as its own card in terminal and web, kind commit with config key blockVerbosityCommit (default short), following short/glimpse/long (task hyt): short is one row, committed <short hash> <subject> (amended for an amend) in the commit color; glimpse and long add the files with +/- counts, then the lines. Clients watching the session therefore see each commit in the transcript; other clients keep the commit notice of task hy. Its lines are those commits.committed gives (task sg0), which the model already gets in the Bash result. Commits Hal did not observe in a Bash call get no card.',
	status: 'planned',
	needs: ['hy', 'hyt', '9p', 'sg0'],
	foldInto: ['hy']
